Read and follow the repository-root AGENTS.md before working in this project. It is the canonical project contract, mirrored byte-for-byte in CLAUDE.md. In particular, follow its human-rating file placement rule: track actual ratings in experiments/09_prepare_packets_and_collect_human_ratings/ratings/ and combine named data links and dated completion status in that experiment’s README.md. For every participant, active personal Google Sheets and current CSV forms/exports begin with `rater_name,rater_id`, with the actual roster-verified name prefilled in each item row. Match CSV snapshots to live table headers and values; import/export by field name. Preserve stable IDs, source responses, assignments and immutable archives; never invent missing scores or attestations. Raw `ratings/` files also preserve exact case, spelling, blanks and N/A rows; never fill response fields with assistant timing estimates, background, confidence or rationale. Keep normalized analysis exports in `ratings/analysis/`, and use `RAW_MANIFEST.json` for current raw source hashes. Use names in rating filenames. Do not ignore, anonymize or relocate these authorized collection deliverables by default. This repository owns collection; related VeriBench, cert-judge and correlation-versus-loss projects link to this workflow.

@AGENTS.md


User decision, 11-11-2026: participant instructions, current CSV forms and their corresponding Google Sheets must use the same exact field names, order, meanings, answer options and required/optional status. Use `experiments/09_prepare_packets_and_collect_human_ratings/support/RATING_FORM_SCHEMA.json` as the shared schema and `experiments/09_prepare_packets_and_collect_human_ratings/rating_task_packet/RATING_FORM_GUIDE.md` as the field guide. Keep checklist questions in the unchanged scoring rubric, not as entry columns; retain historical header aliases for imports. Before dispatch or publishing a revision, compare the packet CSV header with the live sheet header and verify every field against the guide. Preserve the scoring rubric, score scale, spam adjustment, uncertainty rule and scientific payload. Never rewrite delivered packets or historical snapshots: create a versioned replacement, record old/new hashes and wording, and refresh the corresponding current CSV, raw manifest, index and status together. A future template does not establish that an existing participant has received it. Keep revisions identifiable by participant and round.

Editable human-rating task files belong in `experiments/09_prepare_packets_and_collect_human_ratings/rating_task_packet/`. Preserve Brando’s drafts. Keep coordinator/import/design records and tests in `support/`; keep collected raw ratings in `ratings/`. Do not rewrite delivered packets during layout cleanup.

## Use descriptive names

User decision, 11-11-2026: use plain, descriptive names for project files, folders, sheet columns, instructions and user-facing labels. Prefer `warm_up_01_median` to `W01`, `agent_1.lean` to `W01a.lean`, and the full checklist question to a bare `A`, `B`, `C` or `D`. Spell out meaningful words rather than shortening them to save space; a reader should understand a name without a separate decoding guide. Keep anonymous candidate names neutral so they do not reveal solver identities. Retain stable historical IDs where needed for joins and provenance, but explain their purpose and provide descriptive names alongside them. When renaming, update instructions, CSV forms, Google Sheets, links and builders together, preserve file contents and delivered archives, and record the old-to-new mapping. This rule does not authorize unrelated bulk renames or changes to scoring.

## Keep the project uncluttered

User decision, 11-11-2026: use the fewest files needed for the actual workflow. Put links and short instructions in an existing entry document instead of creating one-link text files, duplicate guides or additional summaries. The personal rating Sheet link belongs in `rating_task_packet/START_HERE.md`; do not recreate `YOUR_RATING_SHEET.txt` in current working or newly generated packets. Keep one editable home for each document, update its consumers when consolidating, and remove redundant working files once their useful content is retained. Keep maintenance/history in `support/`; preserve raw responses, unique provenance and already-delivered archives.

## Current human-rating form — user decision, 11-11-2026

Warm-up and main forms share eleven columns: five prefilled identifiers (`rater_name`, `rater_id`, `item_id`, `task_folder`, `candidate_file`), `final_score_0_5`, `ai_assisted_rating_attestation`, `minutes_spent`, `confidence`, `rationale`, and `problem_note`. Show the name and candidate path; hide internal keys B:D in Sheets while retaining them in CSVs. Score and attestation are required for newly completed ratings; request a rough per-file time estimate and confidence. Rationale is preferred but optional; problem notes are conditional on an issue. Keep the scoring checklist/base score in the rubric, not as form columns. Preserve all historical responses verbatim, including restored context; never invent times, confidence, rationales or attestations. The [schema](../../experiments/09_prepare_packets_and_collect_human_ratings/support/RATING_FORM_SCHEMA.json) and participant guide are the current contract. Earlier required-only layouts are superseded.

Use one `ai_assisted_rating_attestation` field: localized AI use plus personal comparison/scoring, not a separate assistance report. AI use is required prospectively; historical AI use remains unknown. Never translate an earlier independence-only answer into this new attestation. Historical ratings remain valid records under their original conditions.

Collection layout under `experiments/09_prepare_packets_and_collect_human_ratings/`: only `README.md`, `rating_task_packet/`, `ratings/` and `support/` at the top level. The README combines task links, named data links and completion status; do not recreate separate top-level index or status files. The editable task lives in `rating_task_packet/`; collected responses remain in `ratings/`. Maintenance scripts, tests and administrative/provenance records live in `support/scripts/`, `support/tests/` and `support/`. Keep one editable packet home.
